WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.080
Next Generation Sequencing or NGS is a&nbsp;
powerful platform that has enabled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.080
sequencing of thousands to millions&nbsp;
of DNA molecules simultaneously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.520
This powerful tool is revolutionizing&nbsp;
fields such as personalized medicine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.240
genetic diseases, and clinical diagnostics&nbsp;
by offering a high throughput option with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.520
capability to sequence multiple&nbsp;
individuals at the same time.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.720
Sanger Sequencing, first developed in the 1900s,&nbsp;
is the gold standard for DNA sequencing and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.840
is still used today extensively for routine&nbsp;
sequencing applications and to validate NGS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.320
data. It utilizes a high fidelity DNA-dependent&nbsp;
polymerase to generate a complimentary copy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:53.040
a single stranded DNA template. In each reaction&nbsp;
a single primer, complementary to the template,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.720
initiates a DNA synthesis reaction&nbsp;
from its 3’ end. Deoxynucleotides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.960
or simply nucleotides are added one after&nbsp;
the other in a template-dependent manner.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.840
Each reaction also contains a mixture of four&nbsp;
di-deoxynucleotides, one for each DNA base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.000
These di-deoxynucleotides resemble the DNA&nbsp;
monomers enough to allow incorporation into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.600
the growing strand, however, they differ&nbsp;
from natural deoxynucleotides in two ways:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.000
1) they lack a 3’ hydroxyl group which is&nbsp;
required for further DNA extension resulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
in chain termination once incorporated in the DNA&nbsp;
molecule, and 2) each di-deoxynucleotide has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.800
unique fluorescent dye attached to it allowing&nbsp;
for automatic detection of the DNA sequence.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.520
As a result, many copies of different-length&nbsp;
DNA fragments are generated in each reaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.280
terminated at all of the nucleotide&nbsp;
positions of the template molecule by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.360
one of the di-deoxynucleotides. The reaction&nbsp;
mixtures are loaded on the sequencing machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.360
either manually onto slab gels or&nbsp;
automatically with capillaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.960
and are electrophoresed to separate the&nbsp;
DNA molecules by size. The DNA sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.680
is read through the fluorescent emission of the&nbsp;
di-deoxynucleotide as it flows through the gel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.680
Modern day Sanger Sequencing instruments use&nbsp;
capillary based automated electrophoresis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.240
which typically analyzes 8–96&nbsp;
sequencing reactions simultaneously.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.840
Next Generation Sequencing systems have been&nbsp;
introduced in the past decade that allow for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.920
massively parallel sequencing reactions. These&nbsp;
systems are capable of analyzing millions or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.960
even billions of sequencing reactions at the&nbsp;
same time. Although different machines have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.600
been developed with various differing technical&nbsp;
details, they all share some common features.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.000
1) Sample Preparation: All Next&nbsp;
Generation Sequencing platforms require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.960
a library obtained either by amplification&nbsp;
or ligation with custom adapter sequences.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.920
2) Sequencing machines: Each library&nbsp;
fragment is amplified on a solid surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.480
with covalently attached DNA linkers&nbsp;
that hybridize the library adapters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.040
This amplification creates clusters of DNA,&nbsp;
each originating from a single library fragment;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.320
each cluster will act as an&nbsp;
individual sequencing reaction. And,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.400
3) Data output: Each machine provides the&nbsp;
raw data at the end of the sequencing run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.000
This raw data is a collection of DNA&nbsp;
sequences that were generated at each cluster.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.360
The differences between the different Next&nbsp;
Generation Sequencing platforms lie mainly in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.600
technical details of the sequencing reaction and&nbsp;
can be categorized in 4 groups: pyrosequencing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.480
sequencing by synthesis, sequencing by&nbsp;
ligation, and ion semiconductor sequencing.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.560
In pyrosequencing, the sequencing reaction is&nbsp;
monitored through the release of a pyrophosphate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.000
during each nucleotide incorporation. The&nbsp;
released pyrophosphate is used in a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.160
of chemical reactions resulting in the generation&nbsp;
of light. Light emission is detected by a camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.920
which records the appropriate sequence of the&nbsp;
cluster. The sequencing proceeds by incubating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.880
one base at a time, measuring the light emission&nbsp;
(if any), degrading the unincorporated bases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:22.480
and then the addition of another base. This&nbsp;
technology is capable of generating large read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:28.720
lengths, much comparable to the read length of&nbsp;
Sanger Sequencing. However, high reagent cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.600
and high error rate over strings of 6 or more&nbsp;
homopolymers have reduced its applications.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.920
Sequencing by synthesis utilizes the step-by-step&nbsp;
incorporation of reversibly fluorescent and&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.200
terminated nucleotides for DNA sequencing&nbsp;
and is used by the Illumina NGS platforms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.680
All four nucleotides are added to the sequencing&nbsp;
chip at the same time and after nucleotide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.520
incorporation, the remaining DNA bases are&nbsp;
washed away. The fluorescent signal is read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:04.000
at each cluster and the terminator group are then&nbsp;
cleaved and washed away. This process is repeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.760
until the sequencing reaction is complete. This&nbsp;
system is able to overcome the disadvantages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.680
the pyrosequencing system by only incorporating&nbsp;
a single nucleotide at a time, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.680
as the sequencing reaction proceeds, the error&nbsp;
rate of the machine also increases. This is due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.480
to incomplete removal of the fluorescent signal&nbsp;
which leads to higher background noise levels.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.720
Sequencing by ligation is different from the&nbsp;
other two methods since it does not utilize a DNA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:40.080
polymerase to incorporate nucleotides. Instead,&nbsp;
it relies on 16 8-mer oligonucleotide probes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.040
each with one of 4 fluorescent dyes attached&nbsp;
to its 5’ end that are ligated to one another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.720
Each 8-mer consists of two probe specific bases,&nbsp;
and six degenerate bases. The sequencing reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.000
commences by binding of the primer to the adapter&nbsp;
sequence and then hybridization of the appropriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.600
probe. This hybridization of the probe is&nbsp;
guided by the two probe specific bases and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.480
upon annealing, is ligated to the primer sequence&nbsp;
through a DNA ligase. Unbound oligonucleotides are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:17.680
washed away, the signal is detected and recorded.&nbsp;
After that, the fluorescent signal, along with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:24.640
last 3 bases of the 8-mer probe, are cleaved, and&nbsp;
then the next cycle commences. After approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:31.040
7 cycles of ligation the DNA strand is denatured&nbsp;
and another sequencing primer, offset by one base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:37.440
from the previous primer, is used to repeat these&nbsp;
steps – in total 5 sequencing primers are used.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.040
The major disadvantage of this technology is&nbsp;
the very short sequencing reads generated.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.760
Ion semiconductor sequencing utilizes the release&nbsp;
of hydrogen ions during the sequencing reaction to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:56.080
detect the sequence of a cluster. Each cluster is&nbsp;
located directly above a semiconductor transistor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:02.400
which is capable of detecting changes in the pH&nbsp;
of the solution. During nucleotide incorporation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.520
a single hydrogen ion is released into the&nbsp;
solution and it is detected by the semiconductor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:13.760
The sequencing reaction itself proceeds similarly&nbsp;
to pyrosequencing, but at a fraction of the cost.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.440
In order to be able to showcase and compare the&nbsp;
different technical aspects of each of the above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.320
technologies, the number of coverage that each&nbsp;
run generates when sequencing the whole human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:31.120
mouse, Arabidopsis thaliana, and E. coli&nbsp;
genome are calculated and presented here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:36.160
The presented data is based on the most&nbsp;
powerful machines of each technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.760
For whole genome sequencing data to&nbsp;
be useful a minimum of 30x coverage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:48.480
is required. As it can be seen, the pyrosequncing&nbsp;
method is only able to sequence the E. coli genome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.160
at enough coverage to result in valid data.&nbsp;
The sequencing by synthesis method, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:59.040
the most popular method currently on the market,&nbsp;
is able to generate hundreds of coverage per run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.840
In fact, with this machine it is possible&nbsp;
to sequence 15 individuals within 3.5 days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:11.840
The sequencing by ligation method also generates&nbsp;
enough coverage for all genomes to be used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.480
however, it isn’t capable of&nbsp;
generating nearly as much output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:22.640
as the illumina HiSeq machines. The Ion proton&nbsp;
machine is used mostly in clinical setting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.240
because it is able to provide a&nbsp;
sufficient size output within 2 hours.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.880
In conclusion, NGS technologies are&nbsp;
increasingly being adopted by researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.600
and are bestowing various benefits in terms&nbsp;
of massive parallel cyclic array sequencing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.280
The common advantage of all the platforms is&nbsp;
the quantity and quality of large amount of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:50.480
generated. The decreasing costs of DNA sequencing&nbsp;
by many orders are fascinating. Given the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:56.320
status of certain challenges associated with&nbsp;
NGS it is anticipated that NGS technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.600
will become as widespread as microarray. It&nbsp;
is also expected that the challenges will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.240
shift from mastering the technology to how&nbsp;
best it can go for extracting biologically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.080
meaningful information from large amounts of&nbsp;
data generated by NGS. It is quite obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:21.840
that these technologies are incredibly promising&nbsp;
in terms of research and clinical application.

